package cajeta.xpu;

/**
 * The reach of a kernel-side atomic: which observers its ordering and its result are
 * published to. An optional, **compile-time-constant** trailing argument after the
 * atomic's {@link MemoryOrder}: `flag.atomicExchange(0, 1, MemoryOrder.Release,
 * MemoryScope.System)`. Omitting it keeps the storage's own scope, which is the
 * right one almost always: {@link #Device} for a {@link KernelBuffer}, {@link
 * #Workgroup} for a `shared` array.
 *
 * Like {@link MemoryOrder}, the **ordinal is the contract**: the kernel lowerer maps
 * it to an LLVM sync scope. Do not reorder.
 *
 * The scopes (narrowest → widest):
 *   - {@link #Workgroup} — the work-items of one workgroup. For a buffer only one
 *     workgroup touches.
 *   - {@link #Device} — every work-item on the device. The default for a
 *     `KernelBuffer` atomic, as CUDA's and HIP's `atomicAdd` are.
 *   - {@link #System} — the host and every peer device as well. Needed in one case
 *     only: a kernel that signals the host through a `MemoryKind.Pinned` or
 *     `MemoryKind.Unified` buffer while it is still running. It is CUDA's
 *     `atomicAdd_system`. A host that reads after `sync()` needs nothing more,
 *     because the kernel boundary already publishes what the launch wrote.
 *
 * BACKENDS. nvptx emits `.cta`, `.gpu` and `.sys`, amdgpu the `workgroup`, `agent`
 * and system sync scopes. Vulkan accepts no shader scope wider than Device, so
 * there `System` lowers to Device. The cpu backend has one observer set, so the
 * scope changes nothing there.
 */
public enum MemoryScope {
    Workgroup, // 0
    Device,    // 1
    System     // 2
}
